# Line endings and file modes, decided once for three platforms.
#
# Without this file, a checkout on Windows can rewrite every text file to CRLF, and the
# byte-for-byte autostart tests, the prepack guard and the schema file all read those
# bytes. A repository that passes on Linux and fails on Windows for that reason alone is
# exactly the accidental platform assumption this project does not want.
#
# `eol=lf` in the working tree on every platform, including Windows. Nothing this
# repository ships or generates needs CRLF: the autostart units are written by
# `writeFileSync` at install time rather than checked in, and a `.cmd` file that Git
# has rewritten is a file whose bytes this project no longer controls.

# Normalise every text file to LF in the repository and in the working tree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Never touched by a checkout. These are either binary or files whose exact bytes are
# part of a contract.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.jpg binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.db binary
*.db-wal binary
*.db-shm binary

# The generated-configuration surface. Nothing here is checked in today, and the
# `-text` is a guard: if one is ever added, a checkout must not rewrite the interpreter's
# file. A `cmd.exe` batch file, a systemd unit and a launchd property list are each read
# by a different interpreter, and a CRLF introduced by a checkout is a change to what
# that interpreter sees.
*.cmd -text
*.bat -text
*.service -text
*.plist -text

# Source and configuration that is compared as text, where CRLF would be a real defect.
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.mts text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.jsonl text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Build output and dependencies. Not text, not ours, and `node_modules` is not tracked
# in any case; the rule keeps a future submodule or vendored tree from being rewritten.
dist/** -text
node_modules/** -text
test-results/** -text
coverage/** -text
